\description{
Builds evidence-based prompts from raw open-ended texts grouped by a
categorical variable and optionally sends these prompts to a large language
model.
}
\details{
The function can either:
\itemize{
\item place all groups in one prompt to support direct comparison;
\item create one separate prompt for each group to obtain more focused
group-level interpretations.
}

Missing and empty texts are removed before the prompts are constructed.
When requested, texts can be sampled independently within each group to
reduce prompt length.

By default, \code{generate = FALSE}, so no language model is called and the
constructed prompt or prompts can be inspected before generation.
\subsection{Purpose of the analysis}{

\code{nail_textual()} assists with the interpretation of open-ended texts whose
authors or observations have been assigned to groups.

The function does not fit a statistical textual model. It organizes raw
texts into prompts that ask a language model to identify recurring themes,
contrasts, tones, and internal diversity.

The generated interpretation is therefore a qualitative synthesis of the
supplied corpus. It should not be treated as a formal test of group
differences.
}

\subsection{Preparation of the textual corpus}{

For each group, the function:
\enumerate{
\item converts the textual variable to character;
\item removes missing values;
\item trims leading and trailing spaces;
\item removes empty textual contributions;
\item optionally samples a proportion of the remaining texts;
\item formats every retained contribution as a separate Markdown bullet.
}

The texts themselves are not rewritten, translated, or summarized before
being inserted into the prompt.
}

\subsection{Group-level interpretation}{

The default instructions ask the language model to describe recurring
patterns at the group level.

A recurring theme should not be interpreted as a characteristic shared by
every individual in the group. Similarly, an isolated sentence should not
be treated as sufficient evidence for a general statement about the group.

The default prompt explicitly discourages:
\itemize{
\item unsupported causal explanations;
\item hidden-motive interpretations;
\item personality or moral judgments;
\item generalization from one individual contribution to the complete group.
}

Neutral descriptive labels may be proposed to facilitate interpretation,
but they should remain grounded in recurring textual themes.
}

\subsection{Combined and isolated prompts}{

With \code{isolate.groups = FALSE}, all groups are included in a single prompt.
This is the recommended setting for identifying direct contrasts among
groups, because the language model sees all textual corpora simultaneously.

With \code{isolate.groups = TRUE}, every group receives its own prompt. This can
improve focus and reduce prompt length, but detailed cross-group comparisons
should not be expected because the model sees only one group at a time.
}

\subsection{Sampling and representativeness}{

Sampling can be useful when groups contain many or very long texts.
However, the interpretation then applies only to the sampled corpus shown
to the model.

A small \code{sample.pct} may omit infrequent themes, minority positions, or
internal tensions. The returned prompt explicitly tells the model when only
a sample of the corpus is shown.

Reproducible sampling can be obtained by supplying \code{seed}.
}

\subsection{Textual data summary}{

The returned object contains a \code{"textual_data_summary"} attribute. It is a
named list with one element per group.

Each element contains:
\itemize{
\item \code{n_texts}: number of non-empty texts available in the complete group
corpus;
\item \code{median_length}: median text length in characters;
\item \code{max_length}: maximum text length in characters;
\item \code{min_length}: minimum text length in characters;
\item \code{evidence_strength}: a heuristic label based on the number and median
length of the available texts.
}

The \code{"textual_data_summary"} is calculated from the complete non-empty
corpus before sampling. It is therefore not reduced by \code{sample.pct}.

The \code{evidence_strength} field is an internal descriptive heuristic about
corpus volume and text length. It is not a statistical measure of validity,
representativeness, reliability, or certainty.
}

\subsection{Custom prompt components}{

\code{introduction}, \code{request}, and \code{conclusion} may be supplied to adapt the
analysis to a particular research question.

Supplying a custom \code{request} or \code{conclusion} replaces the corresponding
default block. The reading guide generated by \code{build_guide_textual()} is
still appended to the introduction.

Custom instructions should preserve a clear distinction between:
\itemize{
\item statements directly supported by the corpus;
\item broader interpretations;
\item uncertainty, heterogeneity, or missing evidence.
}
}

\subsection{Generation and confidentiality}{

With \code{generate = TRUE}, the retained raw texts are included in the request
sent to the selected provider.

Users should review the corpus before generation when it contains personal,
confidential, sensitive, or identifying information.

With an external provider, the sampled texts leave the local R process.
With a local Ollama installation, generation is normally performed by the
locally running model.
}

\subsection{Interpretation limits}{

The generated synthesis does not replace:
\itemize{
\item examination of the raw corpus;
\item assessment of sampling and data-collection conditions;
\item consideration of group sizes;
\item qualitative coding by a researcher;
\item formal statistical or lexical analyses when these are required.
}

The result should be treated as an assisted interpretation rather than as
definitive evidence about individuals or groups.
}
